Hi @KoBeWi, absolutely, something like this

```cpp
void ComponentView::_item_mouse_selected(const Vector2& p_position, int p_button) {
  if (p_button == MOUSE_BUTTON_RIGHT) {
    TreeItem* selection = _tree->get_selected();
    if (selection) {
      /* display context menu which has a rename option, which calls _rename_tree_item as the callback */
    }
  }
}

void ComponentView::_rename_tree_item() {
  TreeItem* selection = _tree->get_selected();
  if (!selection) {
    return;
  }

  // Replaces the current visible text with the technical name, if its set in the metadata
  const String current_name = selection->get_text(0);
  const String technical_name = selection->get_meta("__technical_name", "");
  if (!technical_name.is_empty()) {
    selection->set_meta("__current_name", current_name);
    selection->set_text(0, technical_name);
  }
  
  // Trigger edit
  _tree->edit_selected(true);
}

void ComponentView::_item_edited() {
  TreeItem* selection = _tree->get_selected();
  if (selection) {
    const String new_name = selection->get_text(0);
    const String technical_name = selection->get_meta("__technical_name", "");
    if (technical_name != new_name) {
      Ref<Resource> model = selection->get_meta("__model", {});
      ERR_FAIL_COND_MSG(!model.is_valid(), "Rename failed because there is no valid model.");
      model->set_technical_name(new_name);
      
      selection->set_meta("__technical_name", new_name);
      selection->set_text(0, _get_fancy_name(new_name));
      selection->remove_meta("__current_name");
    }
  }
}

// Proposed change handler
void ComponentView::_item_edit_canceled() {
  TreeItem* selection  = _tree->get_selected();
  if (selection) {
    const String current_name = selection->get_meta("__current_name", "");
    if (!current_name.is_empty()) {
      // Restore fancy name on cancel
      selection->set_text(0, current_name);
      selection->remove_meta("__current_name");
    }
  }
}

String ComponentView::_get_fancy_name(const String& p_technical_name) {
  // This could be any logic, depending on the specific component type being modified.
  return p_technical_name.capitalize();
}

ComponentView::ComponentView() {
  _tree = memnew(Tree);
  /* set options */
  _tree->connect("item_mouse_selected", callable_mp(this, &ComponentView::_item_mouse_selected));
  _tree->connect("item_edited", callable_mp(this, &ComponentView::_item_edited));
  // with proposed changes
  _tree->connect("item_edit_canceled", callable_mp(this, &ComponentView::_item_edit_canceled));
}
```

This was just thrown together based on part of my widget, so I may have missed something, but I think in concept, all the bits are here to at least understand the idea.

I completely understand @KoBeWi. I am already doing something similar to this, and it works. 

But as you can likely grasp, the main issue here is compatibility. While the hack may work now, nothing prevents it from being completely broken in a future build, including a maintenance build, since it works purely off the guise of how the node structure is composed. 

What if we reframe the problem and solution? Rather than `Tree` emitting an `item_edit_canceled` signal, could we provide a way for the component's `Popup` instance to be accessible to the outside world? I haven't looked at the code in `Tree` closely yet, but what I have in mind is like:

```cpp
Popup* Tree::get_popup_editor() {
  return popup_editor;
}
```

At least in this context, there is a standard, universal way to expose the `Popup` that doesn't require tree sniffing hacks and then I could hook into the `visibility_changed` or other signals to handle the niche use case? I think this keeps the upkeep and maintenance extremely minimal on the Godot side, too, right?
